/// Callers can lookup localized strings with an instance of AccountLocalizations
/// returned by `AccountLocalizations.of(context)`.
///
/// Applications need to include `AccountLocalizations.delegate()` in their app's
/// `localizationDelegates` list, and the locales they support in the app's
/// `supportedLocales` list. For example:
///
/// ```dart
/// import 'l10n/account_localizations.dart';
///
/// return MaterialApp(
///   localizationsDelegates: AccountLocalizations.localizationsDelegates,
///   supportedLocales: AccountLocalizations.supportedLocales,
///   home: MyApplicationHome(),
/// );
/// ```
///
/// ## Update pubspec.yaml
///
/// Please make sure to update your pubspec.yaml to include the following
/// packages:
///
/// ```yaml
/// dependencies:
///   # Internationalization support.
///   flutter_localizations:
///     sdk: flutter
///   intl: any # Use the pinned version from flutter_localizations
///
///   # Rest of dependencies
/// ```
///
/// ## iOS Applications
///
/// iOS applications define key application metadata, including supported
/// locales, in an Info.plist file that is built into the application bundle.
/// To configure the locales supported by your app, you’ll need to edit this
/// file.
///
/// First, open your project’s ios/Runner.xcworkspace Xcode workspace file.
/// Then, in the Project Navigator, open the Info.plist file under the Runner
/// project’s Runner folder.
///
/// Next, select the Information Property List item, select Add Item from the
/// Editor menu, then select Localizations from the pop-up menu.
///
/// Select and expand the newly-created Localizations item then, for each
/// locale your application supports, add a new item and select the locale
/// you wish to add from the pop-up menu in the Value field. This list should
/// be consistent with the languages listed in the AccountLocalizations.supportedLocales
/// property.
abstract class AccountLocalizations {
  AccountLocalizations(String locale)
    : localeName = intl.Intl.canonicalizedLocale(locale.toString());

  final String localeName;

  static AccountLocalizations of(BuildContext context) {
    return Localizations.of<AccountLocalizations>(
      context,
      AccountLocalizations,
    )!;
  }

  static const LocalizationsDelegate<AccountLocalizations> delegate =
      _AccountLocalizationsDelegate();

  /// A list of this localizations delegate along with the default localizations
  /// delegates.
  ///
  /// Returns a list of localizations delegates containing this delegate along with
  /// GlobalMaterialLocalizations.delegate, GlobalCupertinoLocalizations.delegate,
  /// and GlobalWidgetsLocalizations.delegate.
  ///
  /// Additional delegates can be added by appending to this list in
  /// MaterialApp. This list does not have to be used at all if a custom list
  /// of delegates is preferred or required.
  static const List<LocalizationsDelegate<dynamic>> localizationsDelegates =
      <LocalizationsDelegate<dynamic>>[
        delegate,
        GlobalMaterialLocalizations.delegate,
        GlobalCupertinoLocalizations.delegate,
        GlobalWidgetsLocalizations.delegate,
      ];

  /// A list of this localizations delegate's supported locales.
  static const List<Locale> supportedLocales = <Locale>[
    Locale('en'),
    Locale('de'),
  ];

  /// Consent screen heading, shown before the first journaling session. Part of the versioned wording of the explicit consent under Art. 9 (2) (a) GDPR: changing it (in any language) means bumping consentVersion. Names the moment rather than asking a question, and stays true when the screen is shown again after a wording change or a withdrawal, neither of which is a 'first' session.
  ///
  /// In en, this message translates to:
  /// **'Before emotely sends your writing'**
  String get consentTitle;

  /// Consent screen, first of three points: its bold lead, followed in the same sentence by consentSendingBody. Part of the versioned wording of the explicit consent under Art. 9 (2) (a) GDPR: changing it means bumping consentVersion.
  ///
  /// In en, this message translates to:
  /// **'Your answers are sent to an AI model.'**
  String get consentSendingLead;

  /// Consent screen, first point, after its lead: what is sent, to whom, and the third-country transfer with its safeguard (EDPB 05/2020 para 64 (vi)). Part of the versioned wording of the explicit consent under Art. 9 (2) (a) GDPR: changing it means bumping consentVersion. The recipients must match the privacy notice at getemotely.com/app-privacy: same recipients, same order, same names. 'Vercel AI Gateway' is a product name and stays as is.
  ///
  /// In en, this message translates to:
  /// **'Each answer goes to our server and on to a language model provider through the Vercel AI Gateway, together with the name you chose or emotely picked for you and the language the app is set to, so emotely can ask the next question in your language, address you by name and write your entry. The provider may be outside the EU; where it is, the transfer rests on the EU’s standard contractual clauses.'**
  String get consentSendingBody;

  /// Consent screen, second of three points: its bold lead, followed by consentRetentionBody. Part of the versioned wording of the explicit consent under Art. 9 (2) (a) GDPR: changing it means bumping consentVersion.
  ///
  /// In en, this message translates to:
  /// **'Never used for training, never kept.'**
  String get consentRetentionLead;

  /// Consent screen, second point, after its lead: what the model provider may not do with the text, and where entries are stored. Part of the versioned wording of the explicit consent under Art. 9 (2) (a) GDPR: changing it means bumping consentVersion. Must agree with the privacy notice at getemotely.com/app-privacy.
  ///
  /// In en, this message translates to:
  /// **'Every request routes only to providers contractually barred from training on what you write and from keeping it after they answer. Your entries themselves are stored in Frankfurt and nowhere else.'**
  String get consentRetentionBody;

  /// Consent screen, third of three points: its bold lead, followed by consentWithdrawalBody. Part of the versioned wording of the explicit consent under Art. 9 (2) (a) GDPR: changing it means bumping consentVersion.
  ///
  /// In en, this message translates to:
  /// **'Sensitive, and yours to take back.'**
  String get consentWithdrawalLead;

  /// Consent screen, third point, after its lead: why the data is special-category, and that consent can be withdrawn (Art. 7 (3) GDPR). 'More' is the name of the app's second bottom tab and must match it. Part of the versioned wording of the explicit consent under Art. 9 (2) (a) GDPR: changing it means bumping consentVersion.
  ///
  /// In en, this message translates to:
  /// **'A journal can say how you feel, how you sleep or how your health is, so we ask first. You can withdraw on the More tab at any time; what a provider has already answered cannot be recalled.'**
  String get consentWithdrawalBody;

  /// Consent screen: the label of the checkbox, which is the affirmative act itself and always starts unticked. Part of the versioned wording of the explicit consent under Art. 9 (2) (a) GDPR: changing it means bumping consentVersion.
  ///
  /// In en, this message translates to:
  /// **'I consent to my journal entries being sent to a model provider as described above.'**
  String get consentCheckboxLabel;

  /// Consent screen button that records the consent; enabled only once the checkbox is ticked. Part of the versioned wording of the explicit consent under Art. 9 (2) (a) GDPR: changing it means bumping consentVersion.
  ///
  /// In en, this message translates to:
  /// **'Start journaling'**
  String get consentAgreeButton;

  /// Consent screen button that declines and goes back to the journal, which stays usable. Named plainly, because the choice has to be real. Also leaves the screen after a consent could not be recorded. Part of the versioned wording of the explicit consent under Art. 9 (2) (a) GDPR: changing it means bumping consentVersion.
  ///
  /// In en, this message translates to:
  /// **'Not now'**
  String get consentDeclineButton;

  /// Consent screen: read out by a screen reader on the disabled agree button, so someone who cannot see the checkbox knows why the button does nothing. An instruction for operating the screen, not a term agreed to: not part of the versioned wording.
  ///
  /// In en, this message translates to:
  /// **'Tick the box above to continue'**
  String get consentAgreeBlockedHint;

  /// Snack bar on the journal after the user declined consent: there is no session without the model, but everything else stays usable.
  ///
  /// In en, this message translates to:
  /// **'No problem. Nothing has been sent. You can still read the entries you already have, and you can start a session whenever you decide to.'**
  String get consentDeclinedMessage;

  /// Consent screen link that opens the full privacy notice in the browser (the notice itself is in English).
  ///
  /// In en, this message translates to:
  /// **'Read the full privacy notice'**
  String get consentReadNoticeLink;

  /// Consent screen, and a snack bar on the journal: the consent could not be recorded on the server, so no session starts.
  ///
  /// In en, this message translates to:
  /// **'Could not record your consent. Check your connection and try again.'**
  String get consentFailureMessage;

  /// Privacy settings, under the journal sessions switch: withdrawing consent could not be recorded, so consent still stands.
  ///
  /// In en, this message translates to:
  /// **'Could not withdraw your consent. Check your connection and try again.'**
  String get consentWithdrawFailureMessage;

  /// Consent screen and Privacy settings: the app could not find out whether consent already stands. Not the same as 'you have not consented'.
  ///
  /// In en, this message translates to:
  /// **'Could not check whether you have already agreed to this. Nothing has been sent. Check your connection and try again.'**
  String get consentUnknownMessage;

  /// Consent screen button, after reading or recording the consent failed: tries the same thing again.
  ///
  /// In en, this message translates to:
  /// **'Try again'**
  String get consentRetryButton;

  /// Consent screen button after whether consent stands could not be read: leaves the screen without answering, since nothing was asked.
  ///
  /// In en, this message translates to:
  /// **'Back'**
  String get consentBackButton;

  /// First-launch sheet heading: the usage-analytics question (PostHog, consent under § 25 TDDDG and Art. 6 (1) (a) GDPR). The companion speaks as 'I'. Part of the versioned usage-analytics wording: changing it means bumping usageAnalyticsVersion.
  ///
  /// In en, this message translates to:
  /// **'May I count how you use the app?'**
  String get usageAnalyticsTitle;

  /// First-launch sheet, under the question: why, in one breath. Part of the versioned usage-analytics wording: changing it means bumping usageAnalyticsVersion.
  ///
  /// In en, this message translates to:
  /// **'It shows which screens help and where people get stuck, so I can get better. What you write never goes into it.'**
  String get usageAnalyticsBody;

  /// First-launch sheet, next to a check mark: what is counted and where it is kept. Must agree with the privacy notice at getemotely.com/app-privacy. Part of the versioned usage-analytics wording: changing it means bumping usageAnalyticsVersion.
  ///
  /// In en, this message translates to:
  /// **'Taps, screens and crash reports, stored with PostHog in the EU'**
  String get usageAnalyticsCounted;

  /// First-launch sheet, next to a cross: what is never counted. Part of the versioned usage-analytics wording: changing it means bumping usageAnalyticsVersion.
  ///
  /// In en, this message translates to:
  /// **'Never your answers, entries or name'**
  String get usageAnalyticsNever;

  /// First-launch sheet button that refuses usage analytics. Exactly as prominent as the allow button: a refusal has to be as easy as the yes. Part of the versioned usage-analytics wording: changing it means bumping usageAnalyticsVersion.
  ///
  /// In en, this message translates to:
  /// **'Don’t allow'**
  String get usageAnalyticsDenyButton;

  /// First-launch sheet button that allows usage analytics. Part of the versioned usage-analytics wording: changing it means bumping usageAnalyticsVersion.
  ///
  /// In en, this message translates to:
